5:[["$","$L10","bns",{"law":{"id":"bns","title":"Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023","short":"BNS","year":"2023 · in force 1 July 2024","kind":"Act","status":"current","summary":"India’s general substantive criminal law, succeeding the IPC subject to savings. This guide focuses on provisions relevant to cyber-enabled conduct.","url":"https://www.mha.gov.in/sites/default/files/250883_english_01042024.pdf","caution":"A crosswalk is not an equivalence of every element or punishment. Establish the conduct date and read section 358 and other applicable savings.","topics":["1.7","1.4","5.5"],"sections":[{"number":"61","title":"Criminal conspiracy","text":"Addresses agreement to commit an offence or specified illegal conduct, with the provision’s distinctions and penalties. A shared technical infrastructure alone is not proof of an agreement."},{"number":"77","title":"Voyeurism","text":"Protects against specified watching or capture and dissemination of private images in the stated circumstances. Apply the statutory definitions and exceptions; do not treat every photograph as the offence."},{"number":"78","title":"Stalking","text":"Addresses repeated following or attempts to contact despite disinterest, and monitoring electronic communication, subject to the definition and exceptions. Preserve pattern, context, and the required conduct."},{"number":"79","title":"Word, gesture, or act intended to insult modesty","text":"Provides the corresponding general offence to the former IPC section 509, with its own wording. Online communication may be relevant, but the statutory elements remain essential."},{"number":"111","title":"Organised crime","text":"Defines specified organised criminal activity and associated conduct. Cyber-enabled financial crime is not automatically organised crime; the statutory ingredients and continuing unlawful activity requirements matter."},{"number":"113","title":"Terrorist act","text":"Contains a distinct terrorism definition, intent, consequences, and exclusions. Read with applicable procedural safeguards and the separate IT Act section 66F framework."},{"number":"127","title":"Wrongful confinement","text":"Defines wrongful restraint of a person within limits. In a digital-arrest scam, assess whether the facts satisfy confinement in addition to personation, cheating, or extortion."},{"number":"308","title":"Extortion","text":"Addresses intentionally putting a person in fear of injury to dishonestly induce delivery of property or valuable security. A coercive payment demand may be relevant; establish the threat and inducement."},{"number":"316","title":"Criminal breach of trust","text":"Addresses dishonest misappropriation or use of entrusted property in violation of legal direction or contract. A data misuse incident requires analysis of the actual statutory property and entrustment elements."},{"number":"318","title":"Cheating","text":"Defines deception and dishonest or fraudulent inducement involving delivery, alteration, or harm-related conduct. Subsection (4) addresses cheating with knowledge that wrongful loss may ensue to the person whose interest the offender must protect."},{"number":"319","title":"Cheating by personation","text":"Defines personation and provides punishment in the applicable subsection. The former IPC section 419 is broadly associated with BNS 319(2); distinguish this from IT Act 66D."},{"number":"336","title":"Forgery and false documents or electronic records","text":"Addresses making false documents or electronic records with specified intent and related punishment provisions. Compare the exact subsection with the old IPC provision instead of treating all forgery as identical."},{"number":"340","title":"Using a forged document or electronic record","text":"Addresses fraudulent or dishonest use of a forged document or electronic record as genuine. The provision and its punishment subsections should be read with the definition of forgery."},{"number":"351","title":"Criminal intimidation","text":"Addresses threats of injury to person, reputation, or property with the specified intent. Anonymous threats, grave threats, and other forms have different punishment provisions."},{"number":"356","title":"Defamation","text":"Contains the general defamation framework, exceptions, punishment, and specified procedural features. An online post is not automatically defamatory; analyze imputation, publication, harm, and exceptions."},{"number":"358","title":"Repeal and savings","text":"Preserves specified consequences and proceedings under the repealed IPC. Read alongside BNSS transitional provisions and the date of the alleged conduct."}]}}]]
